The '''2019 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2018–19 UEFA Champions League]], the 64th season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]] and the 27th season since it was rebranded the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Metropolitano Stadium|Estadio Metropolitano]] in [[Madrid]], Spain on 1 June 2019,<ref name="venue">{{cite news|date=20 September 2017|title=Madrid to host UEFA Champions League Final 2019|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/023d-0e16a85a80e5-62fd034c8ba8-1000--madrid-s-estadio-metropolitano-to-host-2019-champions-league-fi/|access-date=20 September 2017}}</ref> between English sides [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] (in their first and to date, only appearance) and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] (in their ninth overall and their second in a row, having been defeated by [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]]). It was the seventh Champions League final – and the fourth of the decade – to feature two teams from the same association, and the second all-English final (the first was in [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]]). It was also the first final since [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] to not feature at least one Spanish team, with Real Madrid and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] having shared the previous five titles between them.

Liverpool won the final 2–0, with a penalty which was scored after 106 seconds by [[Mohamed Salah]], and a goal by substitute [[Divock Origi]] after 87 minutes. As winners, for the sixth time in their history, Liverpool earned the right to play in the [[2019 FIFA Club World Cup]], as well as against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], the winners of the [[2018–19 UEFA Europa League]], in the [[2019 UEFA Super Cup]], winning in both competitions. They also secured qualification for the group stage of the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League]]. As Liverpool had already qualified through their league position, the reserved berth was given to [[FC Red Bull Salzburg|Red Bull Salzburg]], the champions of the [[2018–19 Austrian Football Bundesliga|2018–19 Austrian Bundesliga]], the 11th-ranked association according to next season's access list.<ref name="uefa1819">{{cite news |title=Champions League and Europa League changes next season |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0242-0e16a85014cb-5ffe48223b95-1000--champions-league-and-europa-league-changes-next-season/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 February 2018 |access-date=27 February 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180227130907/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2499801.html |archive-date=27 February 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Country coefficients 2017/18 |url=https://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/uefarankings/country/season=2018/index.html |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=4 May 2018 |access-date=4 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180511215020/https://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/uefarankings/country/season=2018/index.html |archive-date=11 May 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In March 2018, UEFA announced that a [[Substitute (association football)|fourth substitution]] would be allowed in [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]] and that the number of substitutes would be increased from 7 to 12. The kick-off time was also changed from 20:45 CEST to 21:00 CEST.<ref>{{cite news |title=Additional fine-tuning of club competition regulations for 2018/19 onwards |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0243-0e98912b8e5f-55d54011c9cf-1000--additional-fine-tuning-of-club-competition-regulations-for-/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=27 March 2018 |access-date=27 March 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180327112547/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2546984.html |archive-date=27 March 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> The match was also the first Champions League final to use the [[video assistant referee]] (VAR) system.<ref>{{cite news|date=3 December 2018|title=VAR to be used in UEFA Champions League knockout phase|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/024c-0f8e663255eb-684192980f0b-1000--var-to-be-used-in-uefa-champions-league-knockout-phase/|access-date=3 December 2018}}</ref>

==Venue==
[[File:Estadio Wanda Metropolitano (2018).jpg|thumb|The [[Metropolitano Stadium|Estadio Metropolitano]] in [[Madrid]] hosted the final.]]
This was the fifth European Cup/UEFA Champions League final held in [[Madrid]], after the [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1969 European Cup final|1969]], [[1980 European Cup final|1980]] and [[2010 UEFA Champions League final|2010]] finals, all held at the [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium|Estadio Santiago Bernabéu]].<ref name="Host">{{cite web |title=Madrid's Estadio Metropolitano to host 2019 Champions League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/023d-0e16a85a80e5-62fd034c8ba8-1000--madrid-s-estadio-metropolitano-to-host-2019-champions-le/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=20 September 2017 |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190403234417/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2501768.html |archive-date=3 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The 67,000-seat [[Metropolitano Stadium|Estadio Metropolitano]] is the home of [[Atlético Madrid]], who have occupied it since major renovations were completed in September 2017.<ref>{{cite news |first=Sid |last=Lowe |author-link=Sid Lowe |title=A stadium called Wanda: opening night at Atlético Madrid's new home |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/sep/18/a-stadium-called-wanda-opening-night-at-atletico-madrids-new-home |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=18 September 2017 |access-date=17 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170923194239/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2017/sep/18/a-stadium-called-wanda-opening-night-at-atletico-madrids-new-home |archive-date=23 September 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> Due to UEFA regulations regarding naming rights of non-tournament sponsors, the stadium was referred to as the "Estadio Metropolitano" in all UEFA materials.<ref name="Host"/>

===Host selection===
[[File:Plaza del Callao with decoration related to the 2019 Champions League final.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Callao Square]] in Madrid prior to the final]]
For the first time, UEFA launched an open bidding process to select the venues of the club competition finals (UEFA Champions League, UEFA Europa League, UEFA Women's Champions League and UEFA Super Cup).<ref>{{cite web|date=9 December 2016|title=Lyon to host 2018 UEFA Europa League final|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0234-0f8e35587c90-754ad72cf131-1000--lyon-to-host-2018-uefa-europa-league-final/|access-date=21 December 2018|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA club competition finals 2019: bid regulations |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/uefaorg/General/02/42/99/42/2429942_DOWNLOAD.pdf |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170816234613/http://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/uefaorg/General/02/42/99/42/2429942_DOWNLOAD.pdf |archive-date=16 August 2017 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The bidding process was opened on 9 December 2016 and associations were given until 27 January 2017 to express interest and 6 June 2017 to submit bid dossiers to UEFA.<ref name="Bids">{{cite web|date=7 June 2017|title=Ten associations bidding to host 2019 club finals|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/023a-0f8e541b5f05-02c3f5f2484f-1000--ten-associations-bidding-to-host-2019-club-finals/|access-date=21 December 2018|website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

