UEFA announced on 3 February 2017 that the associations of [[Association of Football Federations of Azerbaijan|Azerbaijan]] and [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spain]] had expressed interest in hosting the Champions League final.<ref>{{cite web|date=3 February 2017|title=15 associations interested in hosting 2019 club finals|url=https://www.aipsmedia.com/index.html?page=artdetail&oldart=20267|access-date=21 December 2018|website=AIPS}}</ref> On 7 June 2017, UEFA confirmed that they submitted bids for the 2019 UEFA Champions League final, with Azerbaijan proposing the 68,700-seat [[Baku Olympic Stadium]] and Spain proposing the then-unfinished Estadio Wanda Metropolitano, which would hold 67,000 spectators.<ref name="Bids"/><ref name="Telegraph-Bids">{{cite news |last=Rumsby |first=Ben |date=3 February 2017 |title=Champions League final to be held in Baku or Madrid in 2019 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/02/03/champions-league-final-held-baku-madrid-2019/ |work=[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]] |access-date=17 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190417223313/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/02/03/champions-league-final-held-baku-madrid-2019/ |archive-date=17 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The bid evaluation report was published by UEFA on 14 September 2017.<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Club Competition Finals 2019 Evaluation Report |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/OfficialDocument/uefaorg/Regulations/02/50/17/48/2501748_DOWNLOAD.pdf |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=14 September 2017 |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180621194212/https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/OfficialDocument/uefaorg/Regulations/02/50/17/48/2501748_DOWNLOAD.pdf |archive-date=21 June 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Wanda Metropolitano was selected as the venue by the UEFA Executive Committee on 20 September 2017, while the Baku Olympic Stadium was successful in its bid to host the [[2019 UEFA Europa League Final]].<ref name="venue"/><ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Nyon meeting |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2498908.html |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=7 September 2017 |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123081032/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2498908.html |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=20 September 2017 |title=Atletico Madrid's stadium hosts 2019 Champions League final |url=https://www.apnews.com/104d12a452294d35b268a2dcf0dcc308 |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=17 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190417223532/https://www.apnews.com/104d12a452294d35b268a2dcf0dcc308 |archive-date=17 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Background==
[[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] reached their first ever Champions League final, becoming the eighth unique finalist from England and the fortieth overall. They were the first final debutants since fellow English and London club [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e99ba5382bd-dba4d65747f8-1000/ |title=Spurs aiming to be 23rd European Cup winners – how many can you name? |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 May 2019 |access-date=16 August 2020}}</ref> It was the fifth time they had appeared in the final of a UEFA competition, having played in one [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] final (winning in [[1963 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1963]] to become the first British team to win a European trophy) and three [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1972 UEFA Cup final|1972]] and [[1984 UEFA Cup final|1984]] and losing in [[1974 UEFA Cup final|1974]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=It was 50 years ago today – our historic win in Europe... |url=http://www.tottenhamhotspur.com/news/it-was-50-years-ago-today-our-historic-win-in-europe-150513/ |website=tottenhamhotspur.com |publisher=Tottenham Hotspur FC |date=15 May 2013 |access-date=4 July 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170812100631/http://www.tottenhamhotspur.com/news/it-was-50-years-ago-today-our-historic-win-in-europe-150513/ |archive-date=12 August 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=1652/profile/history/index.html |title=Tottenham – UEFA.com |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190307112328/https://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=1652/profile/history/index.html |archive-date=7 March 2019 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Had they won the final, they would have become the third English club, as well as the sixth club overall, to [[UEFA club competition records and statistics#List of teams to have won the main European club competitions|have won all three pre-1999 major European trophies]] ([[UEFA Champions League|European Cup/Champions League]], UEFA Cup/Europa League and the now-defunct Cup Winners' Cup).

In eight matches, they had a record of four wins, one draw and three losses in European competitions against fellow English clubs.<ref name="press kit">{{cite web |title=Match press kits (UEFA Champions League – 2018/19 season, final): Tottenham Hotspur FC v Liverpool FC |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2019/e/e_2025486_pk.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190606150315/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2019/e/e_2025486_pk.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 June 2019 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019}}</ref> Of the four ties, Tottenham won two: against [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] in this season's quarter-finals, and against [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] in the 1972 UEFA Cup final, the inaugural final of the competition, becoming the first British team to win two different European trophies.<ref>{{cite book |title=Spurs: A Complete Record 1882–1988 |first=Bob |last=Goodwin |publisher=Breedon Books |year=1988 |isbn=978-0-907969-42-6 }}</ref>

[[File:Команда ФК «Ліверпуль» прибула до Києва, 2018, 4, cropped, straightened, enhanced.jpg|thumb|upright|Liverpool manager [[Jürgen Klopp]] won his first Champions League title in his third final.]]
[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] reached their ninth overall final, an English record, as well as their second in a row, having lost to [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in [[2018 UEFA Champions League final|2018]].<ref name="FIFA">{{cite news |title=Liverpool stun Barça to advance to final |url=https://www.fifa.com/news/liverpool-stun-barca-to-advance-to-final |website=FIFA.com |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=7 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508025617/https://www.fifa.com/news/liverpool-stun-barca-to-advance-to-final |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> They had won the competition on five occasions ([[1977 European Cup final|1977]], [[1978 European Cup final|1978]], [[1981 European Cup final|1981]], [[1984 European Cup final|1984]] and [[2005 UEFA Champions League final|2005]]) and lost three times ([[1985 European Cup final|1985]], [[2007 UEFA Champions League final|2007]] and 2018). This was also their 14th final in UEFA competitions, having played in one Cup Winners' Cup final (losing in [[1966 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1966]]) and four UEFA Cup/Europa League finals (winning in [[1973 UEFA Cup final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup final|1976]] and [[2001 UEFA Cup final|2001]], and losing in [[2016 UEFA Europa League final|2016]]).<ref>{{cite web |title=Club facts: Liverpool |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0252-0e75573bf39d-f79018a82bc9-1000--liverpool-facts/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=2 June 2017 |access-date=26 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180527201409/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=854367.html |archive-date=27 May 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> In twenty matches, Liverpool had a record of seven wins, eight draws (one of which they won on penalties) and five losses in European competitions against fellow English clubs. Most recently, they won both legs against Manchester City in the [[2017–18 UEFA Champions League knockout phase|2017–18 Champions League quarter-finals]].<ref name="press kit"/> The match was the third Champions League final for manager [[Jürgen Klopp]], who had lost both previous finals, with [[Borussia Dortmund]] in [[2013 UEFA Champions League final|2013]] and with Liverpool in 2018.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool reach back-to-back Champions League finals after comeback vs. Barcelona |url=https://www.dw.com/en/liverpool-reach-back-to-back-champions-league-finals-after-comeback-vs-barcelona/a-48641864 |website=DW.com |publisher=[[Deutsche Welle]] |date=7 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508045152/https://www.dw.com/en/liverpool-reach-back-to-back-champions-league-finals-after-comeback-vs-barcelona/a-48641864 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The final was the 171st competitive meeting between Tottenham Hotspur and Liverpool, with a record of 79 Liverpool wins, 48 Tottenham wins and 43 draws. Liverpool won both league fixtures during the [[2018–19 Premier League]] season 2–1 at [[Wembley Stadium|Wembley]] and [[Anfield]]. They had faced each other once before in a European tie, meeting in the [[1972–73 UEFA Cup#Semi-finals|semi-final of the 1972–73 UEFA Cup]]; Liverpool won the first leg 1–0 at Anfield and Tottenham won the second leg at [[White Hart Lane]] 2–1, though Liverpool advanced to the final on away goals, before beating [[Borussia Mönchengladbach]] in the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Champions League final: Tottenham v Liverpool past meetings |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e99ad26ffea-4ea5cecaafee-1000--champions-league-final-tottenham-v-liverpool-past-meetings/ |work=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=8 May 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508224020/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2601484.html |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Domestically, the sides had met once in a cup final, with Liverpool winning 3–1 after extra time in the [[1982 Football League Cup final]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Tottenham: Head-to-head v Liverpool |url=https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=2590&team2_id=1563&teamTabs=h2h |work=Soccerbase |access-date=10 May 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Tottenham Hotspur » Record against Liverpool FC |url=https://www.worldfootball.net/teams/te1924/tottenham-hotspur/records-all-time-appearances/ |website=WorldFootball.net |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508222057/https://www.worldfootball.net/teams/tottenham-hotspur/liverpool-fc/11/ |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Both managers were seeking their first major title with their respective clubs.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2019/05/30/champions-league-final-liverpool-tottenham-klopp-pochettino-trophy |title=Klopp, Pochettino Proof That Trophy Chances Come With Commitment to Process |magazine=Sports Illustrated |access-date=4 June 2019 }}</ref>

The match was the first final since 2013 not to feature a Spanish team, with Real Madrid ([[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]], [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]], [[2017 UEFA Champions League final|2017]] and 2018) and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] ([[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]]) having won the previous five seasons of the competition.<ref name="FIFA"/> It was also the first final to be won by an English team since Chelsea in [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|2012]], as well as the second all-English final, after [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] and Chelsea in Moscow in 2008.<ref name="BBC-Ajax2">{{cite news |first=Neil |last=Johnston |title=Ajax 2–3 Tottenham Hotspur |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48192369 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=8 May 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508233857/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48192369 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Overall, the match was the seventh final to feature two teams from the same association, previously achieved on three occasions by Spanish teams ([[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], 2014 and 2016), and once by Italian ([[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]]) and German (2013) teams, in addition to English in 2008.<ref>{{cite web |first=Karel |last=Stokkermans |title=European Champions' Cup |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/ec1.html |website=RSSSF.com |publisher=[[Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation]] |date=28 February 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110208171501/http://rsssf.com/tablese/ec1.html |archive-date=8 February 2011 |url-status=live}}</ref>

As Chelsea and [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] also reached the [[2019 UEFA Europa League Final]], this was the first season to have multiple finals of major European club competitions featuring teams from a single nation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Has one country ever had all European finalists before? |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0251-0e99afc01f0b-e2b6e033beb4-1000--has-one-country-ever-had-all-european-finalists-before/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Champions League & Europa League: English clubs make history by taking four final places |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48222997 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510040804/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48222997 |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

