===Tottenham Hotspur===
{{see also|2018–19 Tottenham Hotspur F.C. season}}

[[File:Lucas Moura 2.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Lucas Moura]] scored a hat-trick in the second leg of the semi-final against Ajax to send Tottenham to the final.]]
Tottenham Hotspur, making their first appearance in a European competition final since 1984 and their first ever in the European Cup final,<ref name="BBC-Ajax2"/> qualified directly for the 2018–19 UEFA Champions League group stage as the third-placed team in the [[2017–18 Premier League]].<ref name="UEFA-2018">{{cite news |title=All you need to know: 2018/19 UEFA Champions League |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0246-0e98e2deb33b-a3a30f2f14a6-1000--all-you-need-to-know-2018-19-uefa-champions-league/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 June 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180615042440/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2562114.html |archive-date=15 June 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> They were drawn into Group B alongside Spanish champions Barcelona, Dutch champions [[PSV Eindhoven]] and [[Inter Milan]] of Italy, all of whom are former European champions.<ref>{{cite news |first=Martin |last=Rogers |title=Barcelona, Tottenham top stacked group after Champions League draw |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/columnist/martin-rogers/2018/08/30/champions-league-barcelona-tottenham-top-stacked-group-after-draw/1144740002/ |work=[[USA Today]] |date=30 August 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509072339/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/columnist/martin-rogers/2018/08/30/champions-league-barcelona-tottenham-top-stacked-group-after-draw/1144740002/ |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Spurs began their Champions League campaign at the [[San Siro]] in Milan, where they lost 2–1 to Inter after conceding twice in the final minutes of the match.<ref>{{cite news |first=Steve |last=Sutcliffe |title=Inter Milan 2–1 Tottenham |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45551705 |work=BBC Sport |date=18 September 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508221425/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45551705 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> At [[Wembley Stadium]] in London, the club's temporary home, Tottenham lost 4–2 to Barcelona and fell to third place in Group B.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Tottenham 2–4 Barcelona |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45694909 |work=BBC Sport |date=3 October 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509095508/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45694909 |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Spurs drew 2–2 with PSV Eindhoven on matchday 3, played in the Netherlands, but lost goalkeeper [[Hugo Lloris]] to a red card and conceded a late equalising goal to [[Luuk de Jong]] in the 87th minute.<ref>{{cite news |first=David |last=Hytner |title=Hugo Lloris sees red as PSV thwart Tottenham in Champions League draw |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/oct/24/psv-eindhoven-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=24 October 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509070838/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/oct/24/psv-eindhoven-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-match-report |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Tottenham conceded early to PSV in the home leg at Wembley, but two goals from [[Harry Kane]] late in the second half gave the team their first Champions League win of the season.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Tottenham 2–1 PSV |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46087257 |work=BBC Sport |date=6 November 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509000018/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46087257 |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Against Inter at Wembley, substitute [[Christian Eriksen]]'s 80th-minute goal gave Spurs a 1–0 victory and prevented the club from being eliminated.<ref>{{cite news |first=Matt |last=Law |title=Christian Eriksen puts Champions League fate in Tottenham's hands with late strike to defeat Inter |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/11/28/tottenham-vs-internazionale-champions-league-live-score-updates/ |work=The Telegraph |date=28 November 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509070834/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/11/28/tottenham-vs-internazionale-champions-league-live-score-updates/ |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The final group stage match against Barcelona at [[Camp Nou]] began with an early goal for the home side, but a late equaliser by [[Lucas Moura]] preserved a 1–1 draw for Tottenham. The team finished level on points with Inter, but advanced to the knockout stage on head-to-head away goals as group runners-up to Barcelona.<ref>{{cite news |first=Sam |last=Wallace |title=Tottenham complete remarkable survival act to reach Champions League knockout stage with late draw in Barcelona |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/12/11/barcelona-vs-tottenham-champions-league-live-score-latest-updates/ |work=The Telegraph |date=12 December 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509070834/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/12/11/barcelona-vs-tottenham-champions-league-live-score-latest-updates/ |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Tottenham faced German club Borussia Dortmund in the round of 16, marking the second time in three years that the two teams had met in a European competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Tottenham v Dortmund background |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/024d-0e9959daf833-d5bfad45b3d5-1000--tottenham-v-dortmund-background/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=21 January 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190213005846/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2589032.html |archive-date=13 February 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Spurs won 3–0 with a dominant performance in the first leg at home, highlighted by second-half goals from [[Son Heung-min]], [[Jan Vertonghen]] and [[Fernando Llorente]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Jan Vertonghen inspires Spurs to take Borussia Dortmund to the cleaners |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/feb/13/tottenham-borussia-dortmund-champions-league-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=13 February 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190329200427/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/feb/13/tottenham-borussia-dortmund-champions-league-match-report |archive-date=29 March 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The second leg at the [[Westfalenstadion]] in Dortmund ended as a 1–0 win for the visitors, with a goal by Harry Kane early in the second half bringing the tie to 4–0 on aggregate and sending Tottenham to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite news |first=Steve |last=Sutcliffe |title=Borussia Dortmund 0–1 Tottenham |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47442872 |work=BBC Sport |date=5 March 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190407153143/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47442872 |archive-date=7 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The club was drawn in the quarter-finals against their compatriots and reigning English champions Manchester City, with two legs scheduled within 11 days of a Premier League fixture between the clubs.<ref>{{cite news |title=Tottenham face Manchester City in Champions League quarter-finals |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/47580448 |work=BBC Sport |date=15 March 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401195709/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/47580448 |archive-date=1 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Tottenham hosted the first leg, the first European tie at the newly-completed [[Tottenham Hotspur Stadium]], and won 1–0 thanks to a goal scored by Son Heung-min in the 78th minute, following an earlier penalty from City's [[Sergio Agüero]] in the first half that was saved by Hugo Lloris.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Son's solo effort secures win for Spurs after Lloris saves Man City penalty |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/apr/09/tottenham-hotspur-manchester-city-champions-league-quarter-final-first-leg-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=9 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190410131704/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/apr/09/tottenham-hotspur-manchester-city-champions-league-quarter-final-first-leg-match-report |archive-date=10 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Manchester City took an early 3–2 lead within 21 minutes to open the second leg, including two goals apiece for Son and City's [[Raheem Sterling]] and an additional goal scored by [[Bernardo Silva]]. Agüero's goal in the 59th minute gave Manchester City a 4–3 lead on aggregate in the series, but Fernando Llorente scored in the 73rd minute to tie the series once again and give Tottenham an advantage on [[away goals]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Tottenham win Champions League epic as Llorente stuns Manchester City |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/apr/17/manchester-city-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-quarter-final-second-leg-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=18 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510112701/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/apr/17/manchester-city-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-quarter-final-second-leg-match-report |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Sterling scored a fifth goal for City in the third minute of stoppage time, but it was ruled out by the [[video assistant referee]] for an offside during the buildup to the goal, giving Tottenham a victory on away goals to send them to their first European Cup semi-final since [[1961–62 European Cup|1962]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Tottenham oust Man City in seven-goal Champions League thriller |url=http://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=538693 |publisher=ESPN |agency=[[Reuters]] |date=18 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190418130208/http://www.espn.com/soccer/report?gameId=538693 |archive-date=18 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Jason |last=Burt |title=Tottenham edge past Manchester City with dramatic late VAR twist in Champions League classic |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/17/manchester-city-vs-tottenham-champions-league-live-score-latest/ |work=The Telegraph |date=18 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190520014630/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/17/manchester-city-vs-tottenham-champions-league-live-score-latest/ |archive-date=20 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Tottenham faced Dutch club [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]], who had won the European Cup four times. A resurgent Ajax had entered the competition through the qualifying rounds with a young squad and went on to eliminate reigning holders Real Madrid in the round of 16 and Juventus in the quarter-finals.<ref name="Telegraph-Ajax1">{{cite news |first=Sam |last=Wallace |title=Advantage Ajax as they take vital away goal to Amsterdam against disappointing Spurs |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/30/tottenham-vs-ajax-champions-league-semi-final-first-leg-live/ |work=The Telegraph |date=30 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190507053531/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/04/30/tottenham-vs-ajax-champions-league-semi-final-first-leg-live/ |archive-date=7 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Rory |last=Smith |title=Ajax, Unshakable on Champions League Stage, Inches Closer to Final |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/30/sports/ajax-tottenham-champions-league.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |page=B12 |date=30 April 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190511060345/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/30/sports/ajax-tottenham-champions-league.html |archive-date=11 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Spurs, missing forwards Harry Kane and Son Heung-min, among others, to injuries, lost 1–0 in the first leg at home, Ajax's lone goal coming in the 15th minute from [[Donny van de Beek]].<ref name="Telegraph-Ajax1"/> Ajax began the second leg at their [[Johan Cruyff Arena]] with goals from [[Matthijs de Ligt]] and [[Hakim Ziyech]] to extend their aggregate lead to 3–0 at half-time. Tottenham manager [[Mauricio Pochettino]] substituted defensive midfielder [[Victor Wanyama]] for striker Fernando Llorente at half-time, and his strike partner, Lucas Moura, scored the team's first goal of the semi-final in the 55th minute.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Tottenham comeback stuns Ajax and sets up final against Liverpool |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/08/ajax-tottenham-champions-league-semi-final-match-report-moura |work=The Guardian |date=8 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510072555/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/08/ajax-tottenham-champions-league-semi-final-match-report-moura |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Moura then scored a second goal five minutes later, his tight footwork helping him beat several Ajax players after an initial save by goalkeeper [[André Onana]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Neil |last=Johnston |title=Ajax 2–3 Tottenham |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/48192369 |work=BBC Sport |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510130139/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/48192369 |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> After Tottenham failed to convert several chances to level the tie, the match entered five minutes of stoppage time. As the clock passed the five-minute mark, Moura completed his [[hat-trick (association football)|hat-trick]] with a first-time shot from just inside the penalty area to make the score 3–3 on aggregate and put Spurs through to the final on away goals.<ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Rory |date=8 May 2019 |title=A Dream Delivered, and Another Dashed, in One Unforgettable Moment |page=B9 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/sports/ajax-tottenham-champions-league.html |work=The New York Times |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510015738/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/sports/ajax-tottenham-champions-league.html |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Jason |last=Burt |title=Tottenham into Champions League final after Lucas Moura seals another English miracle |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/08/ajax-vs-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-semi-final-live-score/ |work=The Telegraph |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509092309/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/08/ajax-vs-tottenham-hotspur-champions-league-semi-final-live-score/ |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The second leg was hailed as one of the greatest comebacks in Champions League history, alongside Liverpool's semi-final played the day before.<ref name="BBC-Comebacks">{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Peddy |title=Tottenham & Liverpool: Greatest Champions League comebacks of all time |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48163330 |work=BBC Sport |date=8 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190507232205/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48163330 |archive-date=7 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Graham |title='Now it's Moura's miracle': How world reacted to Tottenham's Champions League heroics against Ajax |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/09/now-mouras-miracle-world-reacted-tottenhams-champions-league/ |work=The Telegraph |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509051856/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/05/09/now-mouras-miracle-world-reacted-tottenhams-champions-league/ |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Liverpool===
{{see also|2018–19 Liverpool F.C. season}}

[[File:Divock Origi (25273591916).jpg|thumb|upright|left|[[Divock Origi]]'s brace in the second leg of the semi-final against Barcelona helped Liverpool reach the final.]]
Liverpool, the runners-up in the previous year's final, qualified directly for the group stage as the fourth-placed team in the Premier League.<ref name="UEFA-2018"/><ref>{{cite news |first=Kristian |last=Walsh |title=Does fourth place qualify for the Champions League group stage? Liverpool boosted by this season's rule changes |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/champions-league-qualification-fourth-place-14343601 |work=[[Liverpool Echo]] |date=13 May 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509062819/https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/champions-league-qualification-fourth-place-14343601 |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> They were drawn into Group C alongside French champions [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]], [[SSC Napoli|Napoli]] of Italy and Serbian champions [[Red Star Belgrade]], who qualified through the play-off round and were making their Champions League group stage debut.<ref>{{cite news |first=Ian |last=Doyle |title=The worrying history of Liverpool's Champions League group stage opponents |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/worrying-history-liverpools-champions-league-15092690 |work=Liverpool Echo |date=30 August 2018 |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509062819/https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/worrying-history-liverpools-champions-league-15092690 |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first1=Rory |last1=Smith |first2=James |last2=Montague |title=A Throwback Champion for the Modern Champions League |page=B10 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/18/sports/soccer/rory-smith.html |work=The New York Times |date=18 September 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509063027/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/18/sports/soccer/rory-smith.html |archive-date=9 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In the opening match of the group stage, Liverpool faced Paris Saint-Germain at [[Anfield]] and won 3–2 with a goal in stoppage time by substitute [[Roberto Firmino]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Liverpool 3–2 Paris St-Germain |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45551722 |work=BBC Sport |date=18 September 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508213316/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45551722 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool failed to produce a shot on target during their 1–0 loss to Napoli at the [[Stadio San Paolo]] on matchday 2, which the home side won with a 90th-minute goal from [[Lorenzo Insigne]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Bascombe |title=Lacklustre Liverpool unlocked by Lorenzo Insigne's late strike |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/10/03/napoli-vs-liverpool-champions-league-live-score-updates-latest/ |work=The Telegraph |date=3 October 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508204842/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/10/03/napoli-vs-liverpool-champions-league-live-score-updates-latest/ |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool retook their position at the top of Group C following a 4–0 home victory over Red Star Belgrade on 24 October, including a brace from [[Mohamed Salah]],<ref>{{cite news |first=Emma |last=Sanders |title=Liverpool 4–0 Red Star Belgrade |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45907628 |work=BBC Sport |date=24 October 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190423034142/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/45907628 |archive-date=23 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> but suffered a shock 2–0 defeat to Red Star two weeks later in Belgrade and fell to second place behind Napoli.<ref>{{cite news |first=Andy |last=Hunter |title=Liverpool's hopes hang in balance after defeat at Red Star Belgrade |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/nov/06/red-star-belgrade-liverpool-champions-league-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=6 November 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508204843/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/nov/06/red-star-belgrade-liverpool-champions-league-match-report |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Emma |last=Sanders |title=Red Star Belgrade 2–0 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46087243 |work=BBC Sport |date=6 November 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508214329/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46087243 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

At the [[Parc des Princes]] in Paris, Liverpool were defeated 2–1 by Paris Saint-Germain and fell to third place in the group, putting them in jeopardy of a group stage elimination.<ref>{{cite news |first=Jason |last=Burt |title=Liverpool on brink of Champions League elimination after damaging Paris Saint-Germain defeat |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/11/28/paris-saint-germain-vs-liverpool-champions-league-live-score/ |work=The Telegraph |date=28 November 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508204844/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/11/28/paris-saint-germain-vs-liverpool-champions-league-live-score/ |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool won their final group stage match, played on 11 December against Napoli at Anfield, with Salah scoring the only goal of the game. Liverpool goalkeeper [[Alisson]] saved [[Arkadiusz Milik]]'s shot from eight yards out deep into second half injury time to preserve a [[shutout|clean sheet]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Liverpool 1–0 Napoli |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46511873 |work=BBC Sport |date=11 December 2018 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508224044/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/46511873 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool remained tied with Napoli on points, head-to-head record and goal difference but advanced to the knockout phase on total goals scored, with nine goals to Napoli's seven.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ancelotti, Napoli 'bitter' after elimination from Champions League |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/90minutes/ct-90mins-ancelotti-napoli-bitter-after-elimination-from-champions-league-20181212-story.html |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |agency=Agence France-Presse |date=12 December 2018 |access-date=7 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508060053/https://www.chicagotribune.com/90minutes/ct-90mins-ancelotti-napoli-bitter-after-elimination-from-champions-league-20181212-story.html |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Liverpool were matched against German champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] in the round of 16 and played to a scoreless draw in the first leg at Anfield, mirroring the two sides' semi-final tie in the [[1980–81 European Cup]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Andy |last=Hunter |title=Liverpool and Bayern Munich trade blows but draw leaves tie poised |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/feb/19/liverpool-bayern-munich-champions-league-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=19 February 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508215252/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/feb/19/liverpool-bayern-munich-champions-league-match-report |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> They advanced to the quarter-finals by defeating Bayern 3–1 in the second leg at the [[Allianz Arena]], with two goals from [[Sadio Mané]] and one from [[Virgil van Dijk]] in the second half.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Sadio Mané and Virgil van Dijk take Liverpool past Bayern Munich |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/mar/13/sadio-mane-and-virgil-van-dijk-take-liverpool-past-bayern-munich |work=The Guardian |date=13 March 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190410104343/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/mar/13/sadio-mane-and-virgil-van-dijk-take-liverpool-past-bayern-munich |archive-date=10 April 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool won their quarter-final tie against Portuguese club [[FC Porto|Porto]] with an aggregate score of 6–1, winning 2–0 in the first leg at home and 4–1 away at the [[Estádio do Dragão]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Steve |last=Sutcliffe |title=FC Porto 1–4 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47953686 |work=BBC Sport |date=17 April 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190513184211/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47953686 |archive-date=13 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In the semi-finals, Liverpool faced tournament favourites Barcelona. Former Liverpool forwards [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] and [[Philippe Coutinho]] were playing against their old club for the first time competitively since being sold to Barça for [[List of most expensive association football transfers|record transfer fees]] in 2014 and 2018, respectively.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.hitc.com/en-gb/2019/05/11/liverpool-barcelona-merson-suarez-coutinho/|title=Liverpool alumni Luis Suarez and Philippe Coutinho knew they were in trouble, claims Paul Merson|date=11 May 2019}}</ref> Barcelona took advantage of several missed chances from Liverpool's strikers and won 3–0 at home, with two second-half goals by [[Lionel Messi]], including a {{convert|25|yd|m|adj=mid}} free kick in the 82nd minute, his 600th goal for the club.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Lionel Messi magic puts Barcelona in command of semi-final with Liverpool |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/01/barcelona-liverpool-champions-league-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=1 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508230116/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/01/barcelona-liverpool-champions-league-match-report |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Saj |last=Chowdhury |title=Barcelona 3–0 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48112154 |work=BBC Sport |date=1 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190504064724/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48112154 |archive-date=4 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Despite the three-goal advantage, Messi spoke on the task awaiting his teammates in the second leg at Anfield: "We know the game isn't over, we know we're going to a very difficult stadium with a lot of history".<ref>{{cite news |first=Ian |last=Doyle |title=Lionel Messi was right all along about Liverpool and they are showing it again |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/lionel-messi-right-along-liverpool-26863233 |date=7 May 2023 |access-date=20 June 2026 |work=Liverpool Echo}}</ref> With a big deficit going into the second leg and preoccupation with the Premier League title race, Liverpool manager Jürgen Klopp asked his players to "just try" or "fail in the most beautiful way".<ref>{{cite news |first=Andy |last=Hunter |title=Jürgen Klopp tells Liverpool to shock Barcelona or 'fail beautifully' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/06/liverpool-barcelona-champions-league-second-leg-jurgen-klopp |work=The Guardian |date=6 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508013953/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/06/liverpool-barcelona-champions-league-second-leg-jurgen-klopp |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Despite Mohamed Salah and Roberto Firmino being absent with injuries, Liverpool overturned the deficit with a 4–0 win at Anfield, advancing to the final 4–3 on aggregate, in what was described as one of the greatest comebacks in Champions League history.<ref name="BBC-Comebacks"/> Liverpool's reserve striker [[Divock Origi]] scored the opening goal in the seventh minute, followed by two goals in quick succession by half-time substitute [[Georginio Wijnaldum]] to level the tie on aggregate in the 56th minute.<ref name="Guardian-Barcelona">{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Liverpool stage sensational comeback to beat Barcelona and reach final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/07/liverpool-barcelona-champions-league-semi-final-second-leg-match-report |work=The Guardian |date=7 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190507225631/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/may/07/liverpool-barcelona-champions-league-semi-final-second-leg-match-report |archive-date=7 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Alisson made a series of key saves to deny Barcelona a valuable away goal, a repeat of his performance for [[AS Roma|Roma]] in the previous year's quarter-final as they overcame a three-goal deficit against Barcelona.<ref>{{cite news |first=David |last=Prentice |title=Alisson Becker the unsung hero of Anfield's greatest Champions League night |url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/alisson-becker-unsung-hero-anfields-16241455 |work=Liverpool Echo |date=8 May 2019 |access-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508160437/https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/alisson-becker-unsung-hero-anfields-16241455 |archive-date=8 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Origi scored the match's final goal in the 79th minute, taking advantage of a corner taken quickly from [[Trent Alexander-Arnold]] that left him unmarked in the penalty area.<ref name="Guardian-Barcelona"/>

==Pre-match==

===Identity===
[[File:2019 UCL Final identity.jpg|thumb|Brand identity of the final]]
The final [[logo|identity]] to be used in the final was unveiled on 30 August 2018 during the group stage draw. It was designed by Madrid-based artist Ruben Sanchez (Zoonchez) who drew inspiration from local folklore, including representations of the city emblem, cats (a nickname for Madrilenians), a guitar and a statue in [[Puerta del Sol]]. The colour palette includes blues and oranges that represent a type of Madrid [[sunset]] that is known as a "candilazo".<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Champions League launches 2019 Madrid final identity |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0248-0f8e630e5377-c01a98835ec7-1000--uefa-champions-league-launches-2019-madrid-final-identity/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 August 2018 |access-date=21 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180908061946/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/about-uefa/administration/marketing/news/newsid=2568279.html |archive-date=8 September 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Alberto P. |last=Sierra |title=Uefa unveil Madrid final Champions League poster |url=https://en.as.com/en/2018/08/30/football/1535628710_942838.html |work=AS.com |publisher=Diario AS |date=30 August 2018 |access-date=1 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180901095036/https://en.as.com/en/2018/08/30/football/1535628710_942838.html |archive-date=1 September 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the final was former Spain international [[Luis García (footballer, born 1978)|Luis García]], who played for Atlético Madrid in 2002–03 and from 2007 to 2009, and won the UEFA Champions League with Liverpool in 2005.<ref>{{cite news |title=Bayern drawn against Liverpool in last 16 |url=https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/12/ucl-round-of-16-breaking |website=fcbayern.com |publisher=Fußball-Club Bayern München |date=17 December 2018 |access-date=18 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181218193254/https://fcbayern.com/en/news/2018/12/ucl-round-of-16-breaking |archive-date=18 December 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 63,500 for the final, a total of 38,000 tickets were available to fans and the general public; the two finalist teams received 17,000 tickets each and another 4,000 tickets were made available for purchase by fans worldwide via ''UEFA.com'' from 14 to 21 March 2019 in four price categories: €600, €450, €160 and €70. The remaining tickets were allocated to the local organising committee, UEFA and national associations, commercial partners and broadcasters, and to serve the corporate hospitality programme.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/024f-0e99841d38fe-5bdfdd28dc8c-1000--2019-uefa-champions-league-final-ticket-sales/ |title=2019 UEFA Champions League final ticket sales start on Thursday |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=13 March 2019}}</ref>

Prices for accommodation in Madrid and flights to the city from English airports surged by up to 683 percent in the hours after the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wright |first=Katie |date=9 May 2019 |title=Champions League final: The teams are going but can fans afford to? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-48214483 |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=11 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190511064716/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-48214483 |archive-date=11 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Acres |first=Tom |date=10 May 2019 |title=Champions League: Flight prices soar as Liverpool and Spurs fans seek final tickets |url=https://news.sky.com/story/champions-league-flight-prices-soar-as-liverpool-and-spurs-fans-seek-final-tickets-11715132 |publisher=[[Sky News]] |access-date=11 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190511064233/https://news.sky.com/story/champions-league-flight-prices-soar-as-liverpool-and-spurs-fans-seek-final-tickets-11715132 |archive-date=11 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The handling of the travel logistics and ticket pricing by UEFA were criticised by managers Jürgen Klopp and Mauricio Pochettino, as well as supporters groups representing the two clubs.<ref>{{cite news |date=11 May 2019 |title=Liverpool, Tottenham, Chelsea and Arsenal fans criticise Uefa for final ticket numbers |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/48224809 |work=BBC Sport |access-date=10 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510163449/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/48224809 |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Tottenham announced plans to show a live screening of the Champions League final at their stadium in London that would be opened to a full-capacity audience.<ref>{{cite web |date=10 May 2019 |title=UEFA Champions League Final – Live Screening at Tottenham Hotspur Stadium |url=https://www.tottenhamhotspur.com/news/2019/may/uefa-champions-league-final-live-screening-at-tottenham-hotspur-stadium/ |publisher=Tottenham Hotspur FC |access-date=11 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190511064237/https://www.tottenhamhotspur.com/news/2019/may/uefa-champions-league-final-live-screening-at-tottenham-hotspur-stadium/ |archive-date=11 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Hughes |first=Matt |date=9 May 2019 |title=Champions League: Tottenham Hotspur could show final in new stadium |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sport/football/article/champions-league-tottenham-hotspur-could-show-final-in-new-stadium-sz26jrdvb |work=[[The Times]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=11 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190511064314/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/champions-league-tottenham-hotspur-could-show-final-in-new-stadium-sz26jrdvb |archive-date=11 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Officials===
[[File:Damir Skomina.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Damir Skomina]], the referee for the final]]

On 14 May 2019, UEFA named Slovenian [[Damir Skomina]] as the referee for the final. Skomina became a [[FIFA International Referees List|FIFA referee]] in 2002, and was previously the fourth official in the 2013 UEFA Champions League final. His appointment completed a treble of European finals, having officiated the [[2017 UEFA Europa League Final]] between Ajax and Manchester United, as well as the [[2012 UEFA Super Cup]] between Chelsea and Atlético Madrid. He was joined by two of his fellow countrymen, with Jure Praprotnik and Robert Vukan as assistant referees. Spaniard [[Antonio Mateu Lahoz]] was the fourth official and [[Danny Makkelie]] of the Netherlands was the video assistant referee in the debut of the system at a Champions League final. He was joined by his compatriot [[Pol van Boekel]] as one of the assistant VAR officials, with [[Felix Zwayer]] of Germany appointed as the other assistant VAR for the final. His fellow German Mark Borsch served as the offside VAR official.<ref name="officials">{{cite news |title=Referee team appointed for UEFA Champions League final in Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0251-0e99b2dbe678-45c115988e2d-1000--referee-team-appointed-for-uefa-champions-league-final-in-m/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=14 May 2019 |access-date=14 May 2019 }}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
American [[pop rock]] band [[Imagine Dragons]] performed at the opening ceremony before kick-off, playing a [[Medley (music)|medley]] of their hits "[[Believer (Imagine Dragons song)|Believer]]", "[[Thunder (Imagine Dragons song)|Thunder]]", "[[Radioactive (Imagine Dragons song)|Radioactive]]" and "[[On Top of the World (Imagine Dragons song)|On Top of the World]]", supported by a display of pyrotechnics and fireworks.<ref>{{cite news |first=Richard |last=Smirke |title=Imagine Dragons to Perform at UEFA Champions League Final Opening Ceremony |url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/8510727/imagine-dragons-perform-uefa-champions-league-final-opening-ceremony |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date=9 May 2019 |access-date=11 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190510015457/https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/8510727/imagine-dragons-perform-uefa-champions-league-final-opening-ceremony |archive-date=10 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Medrano |first=Teresa |date=1 June 2019 |title=Imagine Dragons abre final de la Champions en Madrid |language=es |trans-title=Imagine Dragons opens Champions League final in Madrid |url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/espanol/news/article/Imagine-Dragons-abre-final-de-la-Champions-en-13916064.php?psid=85SfS |work=[[San Francisco Chronicle]] |agency=Associated Press |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601201150/https://www.sfchronicle.com/espanol/news/article/Imagine-Dragons-abre-final-de-la-Champions-en-13916064.php%3Fpsid%3D85SfS |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Ukrainian electric string quartet [[Asturia Girls]] performed the [[UEFA Champions League Anthem]] as the teams walked out for the match.<ref>{{cite news |title=Asturia Girls to perform UEFA Champions League anthem in Madrid |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0251-0e99b52f226f-9a8ee59428cb-1000--asturia-girls-to-perform-uefa-champions-league-anthem-in-m/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=21 May 2019 |access-date=23 June 2019 }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
Prior to kick-off, a moment of silence was observed for Spanish footballer [[José Antonio Reyes]], who had died in a car crash earlier in the day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0252-0f8e6c3c76e1-bcc5c6350d4c-1000--moment-s-silence-to-be-observed-for-jose-antonio-reyes/|title=Moment's silence to be observed for José Antonio Reyes|publisher=UEFA|date=1 June 2019|access-date=20 June 2026|archive-date=24 March 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250324031454/https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/0252-0f8e6c3c76e1-bcc5c6350d4c-1000--moment-s-silence-to-be-observed-for-jose-antonio-reyes/|url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool kicked off and earned a penalty kick just 24 seconds into the match when Moussa Sissoko handled the ball in the penalty area, after a pass by Sadio Mané from the left struck his outstretched arm. The resulting penalty in the second minute was scored by Mohamed Salah shooting to the right, giving Liverpool a 1–0 lead and Salah the second-fastest goal in a Champions League final.<ref>{{cite news |date=1 June 2019 |title=Salah scores second-fastest Champions League final goal |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/salah-scores-second-fastest-champions-193637180.html |work=Yahoo Sports |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601205752/https://sports.yahoo.com/salah-scores-second-fastest-champions-193637180.html |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Tottenham held the majority of possession in the first half, but were unable to find scoring chances; Liverpool had their own chances from a series of six corner kicks, but played cautiously with their lead.<ref name="Telegraph-Live">{{cite news |last=Gibbs |first=Thom |date=1 June 2019 |title=Champions League final 2019, Tottenham vs Liverpool: live score and latest updates from Madrid |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/06/01/champions-league-final-2019-tottenham-vs-liverpool-live-score/ |work=The Daily Telegraph |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601183656/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/06/01/champions-league-final-2019-tottenham-vs-liverpool-live-score/ |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> The match was briefly interrupted in the 18th minute by a [[pitch invader]].<ref name="NYT-Live">{{cite news |last1=Das |first1=Andrew |last2=Smith |first2=Rory |date=1 June 2019 |title=Scoring Early and Late, Liverpool Beats Tottenham to Win Sixth Champions League Title |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/01/sports/liverpool-tottenham-champions-league.html |work=The New York Times |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601204041/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/01/sports/liverpool-tottenham-champions-league.html |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The second half featured more chances for Liverpool, including a shot by [[James Milner]] that beat goalkeeper Hugo Lloris but went wide of the goal. Both managers made their first set of substitutions around the 60th minute, with Klopp bringing on Divock Origi for Roberto Firmino and Pochettino replacing [[Harry Winks]] with Lucas Moura.<ref name="NYT-Live"/> Tottenham began pressing their attackers forward and took several shots on target in the last half-hour of the regular time, leaving themselves open to counterattacks by Liverpool.<ref name="NYT-Live"/> Following a corner kick in the 87th minute that was not cleared away by Spurs, Divock Origi struck from inside the penalty area and scored into the bottom right corner of the net.<ref name="Guardian-Live">{{cite news |last=Doyle |first=Paul |date=1 June 2019 |title=Tottenham Hotspur v Liverpool: Champions League final – live! |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2019/jun/01/tottenham-hotspur-v-liverpool-champions-league-final-live |work=The Guardian |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601162627/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2019/jun/01/tottenham-hotspur-v-liverpool-champions-league-final-live |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref> Liverpool won their sixth European Cup and Jürgen Klopp won his first trophy for the club.<ref name="Guardian-Summary">{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=Daniel |date=1 June 2019 |title=Liverpool win Champions League after Salah and Origi sink Tottenham |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/jun/01/tottenham-liverpool-champions-league-final-match-report |work=The Guardian |access-date=1 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190601210213/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2019/jun/01/tottenham-liverpool-champions-league-final-match-report |archive-date=1 June 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===Details===
The "home" team (for administrative purposes) was determined by an additional draw held after the quarter-final and semi-final draws, which was held on 15 March 2019, 12:00 [[Central European Time|CET]], at the UEFA headquarters in [[Nyon]], Switzerland.<ref>{{cite news |title=2018/19 Champions League match and draw calendar |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0241-0e98376f4997-e042439b9fca-1000--2018-19-champions-league-match-and-draw-calendar/ |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=9 January 2018 |access-date=9 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180110054727/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid=2499859.html |archive-date=10 January 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="QF_SF_F_draw">{{cite web |title=UEFA Champions League quarter-final, semi-final and final draws |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/draws/2019/2000982/ |date=15 March 2019 |website=UEFA.com|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190519151818/https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/season=2019/draws/round=2000982/ |archive-date=19 May 2019 |url-status=live}}</ref>

